{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nOn 2026-09-02 the rebar Gerrit host filled its root volume and took Gerrit offline for about five hours. Recovery enlarged the root volume, but that was only an unwedge; the class was unbounded root-disk consumers plus alarms that could go healthy when the host stopped publishing.\n\n## RCA\n\nTwo defects combined:\n\n1. Root-volume consumers had no hard ceilings. Docker/BuildKit, journald, /var/tmp, writable container layers, and review-gate scratch could all consume root without a bounded per-generator cap. One-off pruning or a larger root disk only bought time.\n2. Host-published disk alarms treated missing data as non-breaching, so when the host died and stopped publishing, the alarm could reset to OK instead of paging on loss of signal.\n\nSubsequent measurements showed the original failure was not a single static directory: root usage had sawtooth build/deploy bursts, a rising trough during some merge bursts, Docker cache and unaccounted data, /var/tmp residue, and review-gate scratch originally on root. Later live fixes moved scratch to its own volume and installed per-generator caps/alarms.\n\n## Resolution\n\nThe incident response and follow-up landed the missing-data alarm fix and the bounded disk-ceiling architecture from epic 6202:\n\n- root/data disk alarms no longer treat missing metrics as healthy;\n- review-gate scratch is on a dedicated EBS volume with its own alarm;\n- Docker/BuildKit has configured GC/retention and per-generator alarms;\n- journald has a storage cap and cap-health alarm;\n- /var/tmp cleanup/usage is bounded and alarmed;\n- writable container layers/exited-container debris are bounded and alarmed;\n- the host-wedged/SSM-ConnectionLost recovery path is documented as an unwedge only.\n\nLive close evidence at 2026-09-08T17:14Z: root 16G/60G (27%); /var/gerrit 13G/50G (25%); /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch mounted on its own 50G volume at 20G/50G (39%); Build Cache 2.113GB below its 5GiB cap; journald 1.8G; /var/tmp rebar residue empty. Disk-related CloudWatch alarms were OK: root/data/gate-scratch disk, Docker storage/BuildKit/unaccounted/du, journal cap/usage, var-tmp cleanup/usage, and container reaper/stats/writable usage.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] AC1 [non-codebase]: rebar-root-disk-pressure and rebar-gerrit-data-disk-high no longer treat missing data as healthy; missing publisher data pages instead of clearing to OK.\n- [x] AC2 [non-codebase]: the disk alarm re-enters alarm when the publisher dies, instead of self-clearing to OK and staying there.\n- [x] AC3: the top consumers of the root working set were identified with measured byte totals where available.\n- [x] AC4: root usage has bounded ceilings, not merely faster reclaim: the per-generator cap/alarm architecture above is landed and live, and current host measurements prove usage is below those ceilings.\n- [x] AC5: the /var/gerrit remediation was completed or explicitly carried by the bounded-ceiling work, so the data volume is not left with the same unbounded shape.\n- [x] AC6 [non-codebase]: the wedged-host/SSM-ConnectionLost runbook path records snapshot -> modify-volume -> reboot -> cloud-init growpart as an unwedge requiring follow-up reclaim.\n\n## Close class\n\nenv_integration: the bug was a production infrastructure/operations failure, fixed by deployed host configuration, runbooks, alarms, and landed infra support."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788888643114343001,"uuid":"e6f2a37e-64da-4e0f-b8fb-967068281d93"}